00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:00.410
Allow me to introduce Officer Cameron Perlett and Mr.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:01.660
Michael Fowler.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:05.090
We're here to recognize Officer Perlett and Officer Joel Stevens,

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:08.210
who couldn't be here tonight for the actions that saved Mr.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.360
Fowler's life.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:13.770
On August 8th, Officer Stevens and Perlet were responding to what appeared

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:16.490
to be a traffic accident at Doctor Joseph E.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:18.610
Lowry Boulevard in Saint Clair.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.570
When Officer Perlett approached Mr.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.960
Fowler's vehicle, he found him unconscious and in serious medical distress.

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:28.240
The officers could not get the door open.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:32.870
So Officer Stevens had to break the passenger window out in order to get

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:33.310
access to Mr.

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:34.040
Fowler.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.550
Officer Parrot got inside and found that Mr.

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:38.830
uh, Fowler was barely breathing.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:42.890
Both officers worked to get him out of the vehicle and then Officer Stevens

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:43.450
assessed Mr.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:47.450
Fowler and determined he was no longer breathing and did not have a pulse.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:51.990
So Officer Parrott immediately began CPR while Officer Stevens supported

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:52.140
Mr.

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:55.940
Fowler's head and neck and assisted until a hunt for Fire and Rescue could

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:56.550
arrive.

00:05:57.110 --> 00:05:59.020
This all happened in just a matter of minutes.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:03.640
But in those minutes these officers recognized what was happening,

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:04.840
acted quickly.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:08.790
And put their training to work when somebody was in critical need.

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:12.580
And this evening, we have the privilege of seeing this person that they

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:13.740
were fighting to save.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:17.950
Mr.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.120
Valer, we're so glad that you're here.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:22.550
We're grateful that our officers were able to,

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:24.580
uh, be of assistance to you when you needed it.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.750
Officer Perry, your quick response, your training,

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:32.350
your ability to remain calm in a critical situation certainly made a difference.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:37.140
This is exactly what we expect from our officers when the moment calls for

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:41.540
to recognize what needs to be done and to do everything possible to protect

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:42.740
and preserve lives.

